most anyone.Their accent have a cadence to it and a way of coming out that I can't quite describe except as being almost as half-singing, although it has no pitch accent to it, just the usual stress accent. For many Brazilians it sounds a lot as a con-man, to be honest, but I really find it a beautiful accent.<br /> <br /> As my parting words for now, I'd like to make the place's climate and geography clearer. Despite being known for the heat, it is actually fairly in the south of the Tropical Region and most neighboring places have a climate more similar to temperate. What happens is that its location works almost like a natural pressure cooker, the city lays between a bay and a range of mountains that traps the hot, humid air inside a giant bubble. As a result, summer temperatures easily raise above 40\u00baC (105 \u00baF) and can be as high as 45 (113), sometimes even higher. In winter it is still higher than 20 or 21 (70) most of the time, but for many Cariocas this is more than enough to put on a coat, no joke, some even rattle teeth at this point (I personally find it quite pleasurable and can barely survive there in summer). Some other cities exist in the same general area between the bay and the mountains. The most popular retreat outside the city, though, is Petr\u00f3polis, a mountain city that used to be the Imperial Summer Residence, and has a far colder climate (obviously).<br /> <br /> Just some random bits about the city. I can give more and some for other places in Brazil if you want.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> This was exactly the information I needed, thank you very much.<br /> Paradoxically, I see a lot of similarities in the way of behaving (albeit with the due differences in size) with my hometown, Cagliari, in Sardinia. Even the house full of the team colors can be something that can be found in Italy.<br /> Very very interesting.<br /> <br /> If I can afford, and if you can tell me, also out of interest in obvious preferential paths of many vampire clans, how is the difference between social classes and between favelas and rich residential areas seen by the inhabitants themselves?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26315-querion","name":"Querion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-02T23:47:14-07:00","id":1488944,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Querion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1488781-play-the-rio-de-janeiro-atmosphere?p=1488940#post1488940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> This was exactly the information I needed, thank you very much.<br /> Paradoxically, I see a lot of similarities in the way of behaving (albeit with the due differences in size) with my hometown, Cagliari, in Sardinia. Even the house full of the team colors can be something that can be found in Italy.<br /> Very very interesting.<br /> <br /> If I can afford, and if you can tell me, also out of interest in obvious preferential paths of many vampire clans, how is the difference between social classes and between favelas and rich residential areas seen by the inhabitants themselves?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is partly my perception as a person who spent 10 days there and partly a conversation I had about the issue with a local sociologist.<br /> An interesting characteristic of Rio is that the favelas are on top of the hills, and dispersed all about the city. So, even though there is a class separation, it is more porous than usual. You can see this at the beach, because Ipanema or Copacabana are frequented both by middle class inhabitants from these neighbourhoods and from dwellers of the favelas above","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-03T19:02:04-07:00","id":1489041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Querion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1488781-play-the-rio-de-janeiro-atmosphere?p=1488940#post1488940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I can afford, and if you can tell me, also out of interest in obvious preferential paths of many vampire clans, how is the difference between social classes and between favelas and rich residential areas seen by the inhabitants themselves?</div> </div> </div> </div>To answer this and add to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"27429\" data-vbnamecard=\"27429\">Microcuchon</a>'s observation (it is true), I think it's best to quickly explore the history of those shanty towns in Rio, as it is very particular.<br /> <br /> During the 19th century the Abolitionist Movement was gaining force (finally) and laws that would slowly eradicate slavery were being passed according to a long-term plan by Emperor Dom Pedro II (an abolitionist himself). As the country's capital and richest city at the time, Rio de Janeiro had a huge urban population of slaves living with their owners.<br /> <br /> In 1887, though, Dom Pedro fell ill and went to Europe for treatment, leaving behind the Imperial Princess Isabel de Bragan\u00e7a as regent, someone who had glaring gaps in her education for rulership. When anti-abolitionists almost managed to slow down the progress of the abolition plan, one of the few matters if state that Isabel had an intense interest in, she stepped up and take drastic measures. So much so that in a matter of few days she signed the Lei \u00c1urea (Golden Law), abolishing slavery completely and irreversibly at once, promptly ending every slave-owner relationship.<br /> <br /> And so, while the law itself wasn't a bad thing and the risk of many years of fallback on abolition was real, the city literally changed from having a large population of slaves to having a large population of homeless people.<br /> <br /> By then Rio was dotted by lush hills that weren't suitable for construction in almost any scale. But that also meant they were the only unclaimed land at a short distance, and the slaves hadn't be exiled or anything, just freed. So up they went, occupying whatever plot of free land they could in the &quot;morros&quot;, forming scattered shanty towns in the midst of the city or near it instead of going anywhere far. And since those hills were lush and dotted the city, many with rivulets and rivers coming from them (the city was called Rio de Janeiro both due to one river of same name and the great number of rivers crossing it, although today they're either dry or, more commonly, paved over), more affluent neighborhoods weren't far.<br /> <br /> With time the favelas themselves developed, and started to attract the poor regardless due to cheaper housing and living conditions. Some are entire small or even medium-sized cities on their own by now (Rocinha, the biggest one, has 180 thousand inhabitants, and together the favelas have more than a million and a half in Rio). Being cheap, poor neighborhoods they face much the same problems that ethnically identifiable poor neighborhoods face anywhere. The State mostly failed them royally, they're seen as &quot;breeding ground&quot; for crime and anti-social behavior while the wealthier residents of the city ignore that their masses of employees live there.<br /> <br /> Employees that act as personal servants are very much a thing in Rio from the Middle Class upwards, with people expecting from the simplest house cleaner far more service than simply cleaning the house, unless they have the money to hire specialized, dedicated servants. This isn't uncommon anywhere in the country, but in Rio the proximity of the favelas gives a particular light to it.<br /> <br /> Combining poverty, state negligence and geographical advantage on defense, the organized crime achieved a tight control in favelas to a degree that is completely out of scale. Conflicts between drug dealers are shootings that can be heard several kilometres away and frequently make victims with stray bullets, far enough for some of them being middle class citizens in ground residential areas caught at home. Police operations in favelas are effectively military operation, with the standard vehicle being the &quot;Caveir\u00e3o&quot; (Big Skull), a bullet-proof armed vehicle weighting above 10 tons. It is not a combat vehicle in comparison with an actual war tank, but it is almost the same in a civilian zone. Reported collateral damage in those operations is big, and unreported is said to be far bigger, so while communities usually don't nurture any love for the criminals that control the hills, the police is openly seen as just as much or even bigger a threat.<br /> <br /> Some wealthy residences are built in hills nowadays, but most of those aren't in the same ones as the favelas. Instead they're built in hills whose geography is a challenge even for favela builders, where large sums of money finance the engineering projects to make the large houses possible. But there are large mansions in the favelas too, where the wealthiest drug bosses live and keep their personal arsenals, that frequently include some military-grade weapons and ordnance, national and imported. While state-sponsored social workers are rare in the favelas, research teams and NGOs are a common presence, including international ones, negotiating their stay with the crime bosses and trying to ease the results of state negligence, or simply study the context, the challenges and the means for overcoming them that the locals develop (curiously they attract a number of engineers).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-04T06:40:23-07:00","id":1489104,"title":"","content":"I'm very interest in the topic of this thread, since Rio is a place that should come up soon in my chronicle, as a side-location for 5-10 sessions, and I'm still far from having a decent arrangement for the setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manfr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1488781-play-the-rio-de-janeiro-atmosphere?p=1488869#post1488869\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hi fellow italian!<br /> <br /> Grab this fan made manual from the Storytellers Vault, it's fantastic and writte by a local: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/m/product/261806\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.storytellersvault.com/m/product/261806</a></div> </div> </div> </div>Has anyone some experience with this setting book?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26404-submariner93","name":"Submariner93","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26404_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-04T23:21:55-07:00","id":1489190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1488781-play-the-rio-de-janeiro-atmosphere?p=1489041#post1489041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To answer this and add to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"27429\" data-vbnamecard=\"27429\">Microcuchon</a>'s observation (it is true), I think it's best to quickly explore the history of those shanty towns in Rio, as it is very particular.<br /> <br /> During the 19th century the Abolitionist Movement was gaining force (finally) and laws that would slowly eradicate slavery were being passed according to a long-term plan by Emperor Dom Pedro II (an abolitionist himself). As the country's capital and richest city at the time, Rio de Janeiro had a huge urban population of slaves living with their owners.<br /> <br /> In 1887, though, Dom Pedro fell ill and went to Europe for treatment, leaving behind the Imperial Princess Isabel de Bragan\u00e7a as regent, someone who had glaring gaps in her education for rulership. When anti-abolitionists almost managed to slow down the progress of the abolition plan, one of the few matters if state that Isabel had an intense interest in, she stepped up and take drastic measures. So much so that in a matter of few days she signed the Lei \u00c1urea (Golden Law), abolishing slavery completely and irreversibly at once, promptly ending every slave-owner relationship.<br /> <br /> And so, while the law itself wasn't a bad thing and the risk of many years of fallback on abolition was real, the city literally changed from having a large population of slaves to having a large population of homeless people.<br /> <br /> By then Rio was dotted by lush hills that weren't suitable for construction in almost any scale. But that also meant they were the only unclaimed land at a short distance, and the slaves hadn't be exiled or anything, just freed. So up they went, occupying whatever plot of free land they could in the &quot;morros&quot;, forming scattered shanty towns in the midst of the city or near it instead of going anywhere far. And since those hills were lush and dotted the city, many with rivulets and rivers coming from them (the city was called Rio de Janeiro both due to one river of same name and the great number of rivers crossing it, although today they're either dry or, more commonly, paved over), more affluent neighborhoods weren't far.<br /> <br /> With time the favelas themselves developed, and started to attract the poor regardless due to cheaper housing and living conditions. Some are entire small or even medium-sized cities on their own by now (Rocinha, the biggest one, has 180 thousand inhabitants, and together the favelas have more than a million and a half in Rio). Being cheap, poor neighborhoods they face much the same problems that ethnically identifiable poor neighborhoods face anywhere. The State mostly failed them royally, they're seen as &quot;breeding ground&quot; for crime and anti-social behavior while the wealthier residents of the city ignore that their masses of employees live there.<br /> <br /> Employees that act as personal servants are very much a thing in Rio from the Middle Class upwards, with people expecting from the simplest house cleaner far more service than simply cleaning the house, unless they have the money to hire specialized, dedicated servants. This isn't uncommon anywhere in the country, but in Rio the proximity of the favelas gives a particular light to it.<br /> <br /> Combining poverty, state negligence and geographical advantage on defense, the organized crime achieved a tight control in favelas to a degree that is completely out of scale. Conflicts between drug dealers are shootings that can be heard several kilometres away and frequently make victims with stray bullets, far enough for some of them being middle class citizens in ground residential areas caught at home. Police operations in favelas are effectively military operation, with the standard vehicle being the &quot;Caveir\u00e3o&quot; (Big Skull), a bullet-proof armed vehicle weighting above 10 tons. It is not a combat vehicle in comparison with an actual war tank, but it is almost the same in a civilian zone. Reported collateral damage in those operations is big, and unreported is said to be far bigger, so while communities usually don't nurture any love for the criminals that control the hills, the police is openly seen as just as much or even bigger a threat.<br /> <br /> Some wealthy residences are built in hills nowadays, but most of those aren't in the same ones as the favelas. Instead they're built in hills whose geography is a challenge even for favela builders, where large sums of money finance the engineering projects to make the large houses possible. But there are large mansions in the favelas too, where the wealthiest drug bosses live and keep their personal arsenals, that frequently include some military-grade weapons and ordnance, national and imported. While state-sponsored social workers are rare in the favelas, research teams and NGOs are a common presence, including international ones, negotiating their stay with the crime bosses and trying to ease the results of state negligence, or simply study the context, the challenges and the means for overcoming them that the locals develop (curiously they attract a number of engineers).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Mmm, I understand. It is not very dissimilar to certain situations surrounding Italian organized crime, especially in the Naples areas, albeit not with this level of militarization of both crime and police. It's something I can have a close reference to, anyway.<br /> You were, as always, very kind and detailed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26315-querion","name":"Querion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-05T01:12:58-07:00","id":1489199,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Querion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1488781-play-the-rio-de-janeiro-atmosphere?p=1489190#post1489190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mmm, I understand. It is not very dissimilar to certain situations surrounding Italian organized crime, especially in the Naples areas, albeit not with this level of militarization of both crime and police. It's something I can have a close reference to, anyway.<br /> You were, as always, very kind and detailed.</div> </div> </div> </div>While other common aspects of organized crime and its usual relation with local police still exist in Rio, including corruption of police officials in mundane patrol, the level of militarization and territory control is what really sets Rio de Janeiro apart in this regard.<br /> <br /> Of note, an important distinction here in Brazil is that we actually have two completely distinct police forces with their own functions, rules and institutions, called Civilian and Military police. The superficial distinction is that the Civilian police handle Police Stations as the Americans know it, take care of investigations and so on, while Military police will run patrols and engage confrontations. That's actually far more complex, and the system is actually a leftover of the last Military Dictatorship, during which the Military police was created to be State Vigilance for the regime instead of for the law, and this purpose still has deep consequences.<br /> <br /> They are a Military organization, indeed (although again, rarely with the level of military-like engagement seen in Rio), but the actual police force meant to act within military facilities is instead called Police-of-the-Army.<br /> <br /> As a rule of thumb, I think it is always good, when running a game in another country, to dedicate some time to research how police and the judiciary system work there. Those are two areas where we have a tendency to take things for granted because the purpose is more or less the same everywhere, but those system have actually a lot of purely technical distinctions that are easy to miss, but also easy to understand once you do your homework, and then help for a <b>lot</b> of things to make more sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-08T03:03:34-07:00","id":1492581,"title":"","content":"Im from Rio, if you have questions feel free to ask!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Don't follow me, I'm lost too! But if you do...</b><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/gimmisky/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/gimmisky/</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/gimmisky/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.instagram.com/gimmisky/</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11263-gimmisky","name":"Gimmisky","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11263_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-08T07:09:42-07:00","id":1492594,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gimmisky</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1488781-play-the-rio-de-janeiro-atmosphere?p=1492581#post1492581\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Im from Rio, if you have questions feel free to ask!</div> </div> </div> </div>If you have comments on my observations, I at least would like. While my father's family is from Rio and I've been there many times, ultimately it is the impression of someone from Bras\u00edlia that rarely gets out of it nowadays.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-08T19:57:00-07:00","id":1492662,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1488781-play-the-rio-de-janeiro-atmosphere?p=1492594#post1492594\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you have comments on my observations, I at least would like. While my father's family is from Rio and I've been there many times, ultimately it is the impression of someone from Bras\u00edlia that rarely gets out of it nowadays.</div> </div> </div> </div>From what Ive seen your takes are pretty spot on! Rio is a very interesting place for a Vampire game, especially if you are an outsider. We are pretty bizarre for the outside looking in!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Don't follow me, I'm lost too! But if you do...</b><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/gimmisky/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/gimmisky/</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/gimmisky/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.instagram.com/gimmisky/</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11263-gimmisky","name":"Gimmisky","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11263_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-10T07:48:00-07:00","id":1492755,"title":"","content":"Rio is one of those cities I associate with sunlight, much like L.A or Orlando. But as they said in the recent trailer for \u201cInterview with the Vampire,\u201d in some cities day is when you sleep off the damage from the night before. Rio is also one of the massive cities, which is probably too large for any single prince or Archbishop to rule.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}